Keep invokeNative a function POINTER instead of routing it through uintptr_t.

Sixteen initialisers reinterpret invokeNative, which returns void, as functions
returning float64/float32/int64/int32/v128. The (uintptr_t) in between is there
only to silence the incompatible-function-pointer-type warning; the cast to the
target type performs the same reinterpretation on its own.

On a target whose pointers carry out-of-band metadata that detour is not merely
unnecessary, it is impossible. A capability cannot be built from an integer at
link time, so the initialiser is rejected outright:

    error: initializer element is not a compile-time constant
        (Float64FuncPtr)(uintptr_t)invokeNative;

and were it accepted the result would be an untagged code capability that faults
at the first call. Same shape as SQLite's SQLITE_INT_TO_PTR in a static
initialiser and MicroPython's atexit.c.

